# Every cycle in the unit graph, beside the product of the factors round it. One row per cycle,
# `violates` where one does not lie inside that product.
#
# A cycle of conversions must come back
#   The rule: convert a quantity all the way round a cycle of units and you must be able to get
#   back what you started with. `units/conversions.sqlc` reads each fusion part that carries a
#   factor as an edge from its part layer's unit to its composed layer's unit, so the corpus is a
#   graph and this walks it.
#
# Containment, not equality
#   Arithmetic forces it rather than taste, for two separate reasons, and both are worth knowing.
#
# The factors are intervals
#   `merge-holding-composition` files its month as [672, 720, 744] and says why: "a month is not
#   720 hours; it is [672, 720, 744] and which one you got depends on which month." Three
#   intervals multiplied give an interval, so the most a receiver can ask is that one is inside
#   it.
#
# No document could close it exactly, even at the mode
#   Every cycle in this graph runs through 720, and 720 = 2^4 x 3^2 x 5. A terminating decimal
#   has only twos and fives under the line, so no pair of filable decimals multiplies to 1/720.
#   The nine is the obstruction, and it is a fact about the corpus's own calendar rather than
#   about anybody's care. Proof: `src/proofs/README.md`, entry `cycle_closes`.
#
# The width is the dependency problem
#   It is not measurement error. The month that turns cards into card-hours is the same month
#   that turns node-hours back into cards. Multiplying the intervals treats them as two
#   independent months and spans nine per cent either way; a receiver who knew they were one
#   month would get exactly one. This is the third site in this repository for the same fact,
#   after `merge-holding-composition/compute`'s shares and the composed remainder beside them.
#   So a tighter rule would accuse a correct filing, which is why this one is as loose as it is.
#
# One row per cycle, not per rotation
#   A three-edge cycle is found three times, once from each unit; the walk keeps the rotation that
#   starts at the smallest unit so a reader counts cycles rather than starting points.
#
# No depth bound
#   A depth bound beside the path guard would be wrong, and composition/descent.sqlc's own header
#   says why: *a depth bound is a guess about how deep a legitimate composition goes*. It would
#   also be redundant: the guard already refuses to revisit a non-start unit, so the walk
#   terminates on its own, bounded by the number of units there are.
#
# What a bound would add is a silent blind spot
#   The unit count bounds any acyclic path, and a cycle longer than the bound would be missed
#   with no vacuum verdict and a clean pass. That is worse than vacuous: coverage would still show
#   a row examined and passing.
#
# Probe
#   Put `WHERE w.depth < 6` beside the path guard, then raise it to 20, then tighten it below the
#   graph's own depth. The verdict must be identical in every case and to the unbounded walk; a
#   bound here prunes nothing.
-- units/conversions.sqlc walked until a unit repeats; the product against one.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    WITH RECURSIVE e AS (
        :compose(units/conversions.sqlc)
    ),
    walk(start, at, depth, p_low, p_mode, p_high, path, filing, layer) AS (
        SELECT from_unit, to_unit, 1, factor_low, factor_mode, factor_high,
               ARRAY[from_unit, to_unit], filing, layer
        FROM e
        UNION ALL
        SELECT w.start, e.to_unit, w.depth + 1,
               w.p_low * e.factor_low, w.p_mode * e.factor_mode, w.p_high * e.factor_high,
               w.path || e.to_unit, w.filing, w.layer
        FROM walk w
        JOIN e ON e.from_unit = w.at
        WHERE NOT (e.to_unit = ANY (w.path[2:array_length(w.path, 1)]))
    )
    SELECT w.filing, w.layer,
           NOT (w.p_low <= 1 AND w.p_high >= 1) AS violates,
           format('%s: the factors multiply to [%s, %s] round it, and one %s inside',
                  array_to_string(w.path, ' to '),
                  round(w.p_low, 6), round(w.p_high, 6),
                  CASE WHEN w.p_low <= 1 AND w.p_high >= 1 THEN 'lies' ELSE 'DOES NOT lie' END)
           AS detail
    FROM walk w
    WHERE w.at = w.start
      AND w.start = (SELECT min(u) FROM unnest(w.path) u)
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'conversion_cycle_does_not_close'
